  ],
  "text": "A photo is a count of how many photons landed in each square of a grid, and for how long. Three dials, and every photograph ever taken is a compromise between them. How wide you open the hole. How long you leave it open. How hard you amplify what arrives. Leave it open longer and you get more light, and anything moving smears across the frame. Amplify harder and you get a usable picture full of grain. Which is why your phone does not really take a photo at all. It takes a burst of them, at different settings, inside the moment you thought was one. Then it combines them, keeping the sharp parts of one and the clean parts of another. The image you keep was never on the sensor. It was computed afterwards. Which is exactly what your brain has been doing with your eyes the entire time. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
